<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sejong the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1397">1397</a>–<a href="/years/1450">1450</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as King Sejong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sejong the Great was the fourth king of Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, ruling from 1418 to 1450. He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-worthies">Hall of Worthies</a>, a royal research institute, and under him Korea created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a> alphabet, rain gauges, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clocks</a> and new works on agriculture and medicine. His reign also fixed Korea's northern border on the Yalu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">Tumen</a> rivers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1419, early in <b>Sejong</b>'s reign, the retired king Taejong sent an expedition against Tsushima to suppress Japanese pirates, and campaigns against the Jurchen in the 1430s and 1440s established the four commanderies and six garrisons that fixed the northern border on the Yalu and Tumen rivers, where…</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ulfilas most likely invented the Gothic alphabet for his Bible; Cyril and Methodius devised Glagolitic for their mission to Moravia in 862 or 863; Mesrop Mashtots created the Armenian alphabet of thirty-six letters in 405; King <b>Sejong</b> of Korea promulgated Hangul in 1446.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li><li><blockquote>…names are a king under whom a thing was made and an artisan whose method a scholar wrote down: Hangul was created in 1443 under <b>Sejong the Great</b> of Joseon, and the movable type that Bi Sheng made about 1040 is recorded in the writings of Shen Kuo.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-invented-it-first">Who Invented It First?</a></li><li><blockquote><b>King Sejong</b> tried to settle them as ordinary farmers under the old name for commoners, baekjeong, putting them on the registers and giving them land, but neighbours and even officials went on shunning them, and the name itself became an insult.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sejong the Great</b> of Joseon published the Hunminjeongeum, introducing a new phonetic script now called Hangul.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
